<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Eastern Chalukyas</h1><p class="st-meta">624 CE – 1075 CE (approx.) · capital Vengi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chalukya branch that ruled coastal Andhra from Vengi for over four centuries and presided over the birth of Telugu literature.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 624 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami</a>, having conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vishnukundina-dynasty">Vishnukundina dynasty</a>, made his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubja-vishnuvardhana">Kubja Vishnuvardhana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna-river">Krishna</a> and Godavari rivers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnuvardhana">Vishnuvardhana</a> soon became independent, founding a separate line that outlived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> branch.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Eastern Chalukyas fought long wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty</a> for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayaditya-iii">Vijayaditya III</a> campaigned as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> according to his records. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-language">Telugu</a> inscriptions are among the earliest, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-narendra">Rajaraja Narendra</a> the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannaya">Nannaya</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-mahabharatam">Telugu Mahabharata</a>, the founding work of Telugu literature. The fertile delta and coastal trade supported the state, and temples flourished at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draksharama">Draksharama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhimavaram">Bhimavaram</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the late 10th century the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> through marriage alliances. In 1070 the Eastern Chalukya prince Rajendra became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> emperor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulottunga-i">Kulottunga I</a>, and Vengi was merged into the Chola realm, the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> ruling until about 1075.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/eastern-chalukyas.jpg" alt="Eastern Chalukyas" width="320" height="466"><figcaption>Image: Ravishankar1827 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Chalukya_Maximum_Extent.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>624 CE – 1075 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vengi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kubja Vishnuvardhana, Vijayaditya III, Rajaraja Narendra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu, Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chalukyas of Vengi, Vengi Chalukyas</dd></dl></aside></div>
